ncestor? Not necessarily because if you remove a node, its descendants are gone, and ancestors remain. So it's a set of nodes that is a union of some full subtrees? Actually, if you remove a node, you must also remove all descendants, but those descendants might be removed earlier. So the retained set is a set of nodes such that if a node is retained, all its ancestors are also retained? Not exactly: if you remove a parent but keep a child, that would be invalid, but we prevent that by requiring all children removed before parent removal. So retained set is closed under ancestors: if a node is retained, its ancestors are also retained (because we never remove an ancestor if any descendant is retained). So that ensures the retained set is a subtree rooted at root. So it's valid.\n\nNow implement solve(case). case is the payload object with nodes list and required_symbols list. Return dict with removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids.\n\nLet's write the Python code. Use standard library only.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n1. Extract nodes list and required_symbols from case.\n2. Build dict node_by_id = {node['id']: node for node in nodes}.\n3. Build children mapping: for each node, children list (ids). Also track parent for each node.\n4. Initialize retained_set = set(all node ids).\n5. Sort nodes by depth descending, then id ascending.\n6. removed_node_ids = [] (order of removal).\n7. For each node in sorted order:\n   - Check if node['removable'] is True.\n   - Check if all children of node are not in retained_set (i.e., child.id not in retained_set). Since children might have been removed already, if any child is still retained, skip this node (cannot remove).\n   - Simulate removal: get all descendant ids of node? Actually, if all children are already removed, then all descendants are gone, so only node itself remains. So we just need to check if removing node causes any required symbol to be missing. For that, we need to see if any required symbol is present only on